        <!-- Content -->
        <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
            <div
                class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"
            >
                <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
                    Polymeric sand transforms loose paver joints into a hardened, weed-resistant, ant-proof barrier — but the installation process is surprisingly unforgiving. Get one step wrong and you are left with a hazy, stained surface that is extremely difficult to fix. This guide is your insurance policy against a botched installation.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If you have recently installed a paver patio, walkway, driveway, or pool deck — or you are re-sanding an existing paver surface that has lost its original jointing material — polymeric sand is almost certainly the right choice for filling the gaps between your pavers. Unlike regular sand, which remains loose and washes out with every rainstorm, polymeric sand contains polymer binding agents that activate when exposed to water. Once cured, it forms a firm, semi-rigid joint that locks your pavers in place, prevents weed growth, blocks insects from burrowing, and resists erosion from rain and hose water.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The problem is that polymeric sand is significantly less forgiving than regular sand when it comes to installation technique. With regular sand, you sweep it in, compact it, and walk away. With polymeric sand, there is a precise sequence of steps that must be followed in the right order, under the right conditions, with the right tools. Skip a step, rush through a step, or install on the wrong day, and the consequences range from weak joints that fail within a season to permanent white haze bonded to the face of every paver on your property.
                </p>

                <p>
                    This guide covers absolutely everything you need to know: the tools required, how to prepare your surface, the exact application procedure step by step, proper water activation technique, curing requirements, and a detailed breakdown of the most common installation mistakes and how to avoid them. Whether this is your first time working with polymeric sand or you are re-doing a previous installation that failed, follow these instructions precisely and you will get professional-quality results.
                </p>

                <h2 id="before-you-start-essential-preparation" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Before You Start: Essential Preparation
                </h2>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Check the Weather Forecast</h3>
                <p>
                    This is not optional or casual advice — this is the single most important pre-installation step. Polymeric sand must be applied to completely dry pavers and joints, and it must have at least 24 hours (ideally 48 hours) of dry weather after activation to cure properly. Check a reliable hourly weather forecast and do not proceed if there is any chance of rain within the next 24 to 48 hours. Even a brief afternoon shower during the curing window can wash uncured polymer out of the joints, leaving them weak and patchy.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Ambient humidity matters too. If relative humidity exceeds 80 percent, the microscopic moisture in the air can begin activating the polymer prematurely as you sweep it across the paver surface. The ideal installation conditions are a warm, dry day with temperatures between 60 and 90 degrees Fahrenheit, low humidity, and a clear forecast extending at least two full days ahead.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Gather Your Tools and Materials</h3>

                <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
                    <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 mt-0">Required Tools and Materials</h3>
                    <ul class="text-gray-600 m-0">
                        <li><strong>Polymeric sand</strong> — enough for your project (coverage varies by joint width and depth; check the bag for square footage estimates)</li>
                        <li><strong>Stiff-bristled push broom</strong> — for sweeping sand into joints</li>
                        <li><strong>Leaf blower</strong> — absolutely essential for removing surface residue before activation</li>
                        <li><strong>Plate compactor</strong> with rubber pad attachment — for settling sand into joints (rent from any equipment rental store for about $80 to $100 per day)</li>
                        <li><strong>Garden hose</strong> with adjustable nozzle capable of a fine shower or mist setting</li>
                        <li><strong>Push broom or soft-bristle broom</strong> — for final surface cleanup</li>
                        <li><strong>Safety glasses and dust mask</strong> — polymeric sand dust is a fine silica particulate that you do not want in your eyes or lungs</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Prepare the Paver Surface</h3>
                <p>
                    If you are applying polymeric sand to a brand-new paver installation, the surface preparation is straightforward: ensure the pavers are clean and dry, and that no construction debris, cut paver dust, or standing water remains in the joints. The joint cavities should be clean and empty, with a minimum depth of one inch (25 mm) for the polymeric sand to fill.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If you are re-sanding an existing paver installation — replacing old regular sand or failed polymeric sand — preparation is much more involved. You must remove all of the existing joint material to a depth of at least one inch, and ideally down to the bedding layer. Use a pressure washer with a rotary surface cleaning attachment to blast old sand and debris out of the joints. For stubborn old polymeric sand that has hardened, you may need to use a narrow cold chisel or an oscillating multi-tool with a scraping blade to break it up before pressure washing.
                </p>

                <p>
                    After cleaning, the pavers and joints must be allowed to dry completely before polymeric sand is applied. Depending on weather conditions, this can take anywhere from 24 hours in hot, dry climates to several days in cool, humid conditions. Do not rush this step. Even residual moisture trapped in the pores of the pavers or in the bedding sand below the joints can prematurely activate the polymer and cause problems. If you are in doubt, wait an extra day.
                </p>

                <h2 id="steps" class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Step-by-Step Application Guide
                </h2>

                <div class="space-y-6">
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                            Step 1: Pour and Sweep the Sand into Joints
                        </h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
                            Open the bag of polymeric sand and pour a manageable amount — roughly enough to cover a 30 to 50 square foot section — onto the paver surface. Using a stiff-bristled push broom, sweep the sand back and forth across the pavers, working it into the joints. The key technique is to sweep diagonally across the joint lines rather than parallel to them. Sweeping parallel tends to drag sand along the joint channel without pushing it down, while sweeping at a 45-degree angle forces the bristles to push sand down into the gap with each stroke.
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4 mb-0">
                            Work in small sections rather than spreading sand across the entire surface at once. This gives you better control and makes it easier to ensure every joint is filled evenly. Pay extra attention to T-joints and intersections where multiple joint lines meet — these tend to be deeper and require more material. Keep sweeping each section until the joints appear full and level with or slightly above the paver surface.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                            Step 2: Compact the Surface with a Plate Compactor
                        </h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
                            This step is critically important and frequently skipped by DIYers. After sweeping sand into the joints across the entire surface, run a plate compactor equipped with a rubber protective pad over the entire paver field. The vibration from the compactor settles the sand deeper into the joint cavities, eliminating air pockets and voids that would become weak points after curing. Without compaction, the sand sits loosely in the upper portion of the joint, and the lower half remains empty — creating a weak, shallow bond that will crack and crumble under traffic.
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4 mb-0">
                            The rubber pad is essential to prevent the plate compactor from scratching, chipping, or cracking the paver surface. Most equipment rental companies carry these pads as an accessory. Make at least two full passes over the entire surface in perpendicular directions. After compaction, the sand level will have dropped noticeably in the joints — this is exactly what you want. The joints should now be filled to approximately 3 mm (about one-eighth of an inch) below the top chamfered edge of the paver.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                            Step 3: Top Up and Re-Sweep
                        </h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
                            After compaction reveals the true sand level, add more polymeric sand to any joints that are underfilled. Sweep again diagonally, filling joints back up to the level described above — approximately one-eighth of an inch below the paver chamfer. If your pavers do not have a chamfered (beveled) edge, fill to within one-eighth inch of the paver surface. Overfilling is just as problematic as underfilling: sand that sits above the joint line will bond to the paver surface during activation, creating rough, unsightly bumps that are difficult to remove.
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4 mb-0">
                            Some professionals do a second round of compaction after this top-up step, which is ideal if you have the compactor rented for the day anyway. Whether you compact again or not, the final sand level should be consistent and uniform across all joints when viewed from a low angle across the surface.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                            Step 4: Blow Off ALL Surface Residue (The Most Critical Step)
                        </h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
                            This single step determines whether your installation looks professional or disastrous. Using a leaf blower set to a low or medium speed, hold the nozzle at a very shallow angle — nearly parallel to the paver surface — and systematically blow every last grain of polymeric sand dust off the face of every paver. Work in overlapping passes, blowing debris toward the edges of the installation.
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4 mb-0">
                            Why is this so critical? Because any polymeric sand residue left on the paver surface will bond permanently when you add water in the next step. This creates a milky white or grayish film called "haze" that is extremely difficult to remove once cured. On dark-colored pavers, haze is glaringly obvious and ruins the entire aesthetic of the installation. Even on lighter pavers, it creates a rough, gritty texture on what should be a smooth surface.
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4 mb-0">
                            Spend far more time on this step than you think is necessary. Get down on your hands and knees at the paver level and look across the surface — you will be surprised how much dust remains even after what felt like thorough blowing. Pay special attention to textured or tumbled pavers, which have surface irregularities that trap fine particles. A soft-bristle hand broom can help dislodge stubborn dust from textured surfaces before a final pass with the leaf blower.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                            Step 5: Activate with Water
                        </h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
                            Set your garden hose nozzle to a fine shower or mist setting. Never use a jet or stream setting — the force will blast sand out of the joints and redistribute polymer residue across the paver surface. Starting at one end of the installation, gently water a section of approximately 50 to 100 square feet, allowing the water to soak vertically down into the joints. You want to see the water disappearing into the joint rather than running across the surface.
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4 mb-0">
                            After the first pass, wait three to five minutes for the water to penetrate fully, then make a second light pass over the same section. The goal is to saturate the sand throughout the full depth of the joint without flooding the surface. You will know you have applied enough water when the joints appear uniformly dark and saturated, and a thin film of water sits briefly on the joint surface before absorbing.
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4 mb-0">
                            Immediately after watering each section, use your leaf blower on a gentle setting to push any pooling water off the paver surface. Water that pools on the pavers can carry dissolved polymer residue, and if it evaporates in place, it leaves haze. Move quickly and methodically: water a section, wait, do a second light pass, blow off pooling water, then move to the next section. Continue until the entire installation has been activated.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
                            Step 6: Protect During Curing
                        </h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
                            Once the entire surface has been watered and excess moisture has been blown off, the curing process begins. For the first 24 hours, the joints are extremely vulnerable. Keep all foot traffic off the surface for at least 24 hours, and keep vehicle traffic off for at least 72 hours. If unexpected rain threatens during the first 24 hours, cover the surface with plastic sheeting or tarps to prevent the uncured polymer from washing out of the joints.
                        </p>
                        <p class="text-gray-600 mt-4 mb-0">
                            Initial hardening occurs within the first 24 to 48 hours, but full cure strength is not achieved for approximately 30 days. During this extended curing period, avoid pressure washing, aggressive hosing, or applying any sealers to the paver surface. Light foot traffic is acceptable after 48 hours, and normal use including vehicle traffic is fine after 72 hours. The joints will continue to harden and strengthen over the first month as the polymer fully cross-links with the surrounding sand particles.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h2 id="mistakes" class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    The 7 Most Common Polymeric Sand Mistakes (And How to Avoid Them)
                </h2>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Mistake 1: Installing on Damp Pavers</h3>
                <p>
                    This is the number one cause of polymeric sand failure. Even morning dew or residual moisture from a pressure washing session the day before can prematurely activate the polymer. When the polymer activates on the paver surface rather than exclusively within the joint cavity, it creates permanent haze. Always install on bone-dry surfaces during dry weather. If you pressure washed the pavers to remove old sand, wait at least 24 to 48 hours of warm, dry weather before applying new polymeric sand.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Mistake 2: Skipping the Plate Compactor</h3>
                <p>
                    Many homeowners try to save on equipment rental costs by skipping the compaction step and simply sweeping extra sand into the joints to compensate. This does not work. Without mechanical compaction, the sand particles sit loosely with significant air space between them. When water is added, the polymer binds only the upper layer while the lower portion remains unbound and unstable. Within a few months, the surface layer cracks away and the joints empty from below. The $80 plate compactor rental is non-negotiable for a lasting installation.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Mistake 3: Insufficient Surface Cleanup Before Watering</h3>
                <p>
                    We have emphasized this already, but it cannot be overstated. The difference between a professional-looking installation and a hazy mess comes down to how thoroughly you clean the paver surface before water activation. Most DIYers spend two to three minutes with the leaf blower and consider it done. Professionals spend 15 to 20 minutes on the same area, making multiple passes from different angles, getting down to paver level to visually inspect the surface, and using hand brooms on textured pavers. Be the professional.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Mistake 4: Using Too Much Water During Activation</h3>
                <p>
                    The activation step requires a gentle, controlled mist — not a soaking flood. Excessive water flow does two damaging things: it blasts sand out of the joints, creating voids and uneven fill levels, and it carries dissolved polymer across the paver surface as it runs off, creating streaks and haze in areas you already cleaned. The fine shower setting on most garden hose nozzles is appropriate. If you can see water visibly flowing across the surface rather than absorbing into joints, you are using too much pressure or too much volume.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Mistake 5: Applying Over Existing Sand Without Cleaning</h3>
                <p>
                    Polymeric sand cannot bond properly when applied on top of existing regular sand or failed polymeric sand. The polymer needs to make contact with the paver edges on both sides of the joint to create a structural bond. If there is a layer of loose old sand between the polymeric sand and the paver wall, the bond will fail and the entire joint will pop out in chunks. Always clean joints down to a minimum depth of one inch — and ideally to the bedding layer — before installing fresh polymeric sand.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Mistake 6: Wrong Joint Width for the Product</h3>
                <p>
                    Standard polymeric sand formulations are designed for joint widths between one-eighth inch (3 mm) and one inch (25 mm). If your paver joints are narrower than one-eighth inch — as is common with machine-laid brick and some tightly butted natural stone installations — the sand particles physically cannot penetrate deep enough into the joint for a proper bond. If your joints exceed one inch — typical with flagstone and irregularly shaped natural stone — you need a specialty wide-joint polymeric sand (sometimes marketed as "polymeric gravel" or "stone dust polymer"). Using the wrong formulation for your joint width guarantees failure.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Mistake 7: Ignoring the Curing Window</h3>
                <p>
                    Walking on freshly activated polymeric sand, driving on it within 24 hours, or allowing sprinklers to hit the surface during the initial cure period can all compromise the final bond strength. The polymer needs undisturbed time to cross-link and harden. Treat the freshly sanded surface like wet paint for the first 48 hours: no touching, no walking, no water of any kind. Plan your installation so that you will not need to use the paved surface for at least two to three days afterward.
                </p>

                <h2 id="how-to-fix-polymeric-sand" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    How to Fix Polymeric Sand Haze
                </h2>

                <p>
                    If haze has already formed on your pavers, the fix depends on severity and how long ago it occurred. For very fresh haze (within the first 24 hours), you may be able to remove it by aggressively scrubbing with a stiff nylon brush and clean water, then immediately blowing the surface dry. The polymer has not fully cured yet, so it can sometimes be disrupted and washed away before it sets permanently.
                </p>

                <p>
                    For haze that is a few days to a few weeks old, a solution of equal parts white vinegar and water applied with a stiff brush can dissolve light polymer films. Scrub vigorously, let the vinegar solution sit for five to ten minutes, then rinse thoroughly and blow dry. You may need to repeat this process two or three times on stubborn areas.
                </p>

                <p>
                    For heavy, fully cured haze that has been in place for more than a month, consumer-grade remedies are unlikely to work. You will need a commercial paver haze remover — products specifically formulated with mild acids or solvents designed to dissolve cured polymer without damaging the paver surface. Techniseal, Alliance Gator, and SRW Products all manufacture effective haze removers. Follow the product instructions precisely, as some of these are acid-based and can etch certain types of pavers if left on too long.
                </p>

                <p>
                    In the worst cases — thick, widespread haze on porous natural stone — professional intervention with a hot-water pressure washer or chemical stripping may be the only option. The lesson here is obvious: prevention through proper surface cleanup before activation is infinitely easier and cheaper than haze removal after the fact.
                </p>

                <h2 id="choosing-the-right-polymeric-sand" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Choosing the Right Polymeric Sand Product
                </h2>

                <p>
                    Not all polymeric sand is created equal. The market ranges from budget products at $20 per bag to premium professional-grade formulations exceeding $80 per bag. The primary differences come down to the quality of the polymer binder, the calibration of the sand aggregate, and the specific performance characteristics the manufacturer has engineered into the product.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <strong>Premium brands (Techniseal, Alliance Gator Maxx, SEK Surebond)</strong> use high-quality polymer binders that cure to a harder, more flexible, and more water-resistant final state. Their sand aggregate is precisely graded for optimal compaction and flow into joints of various widths. These products are more forgiving during installation, more resistant to haze formation, and significantly more durable over time. If you are investing in a quality paver installation that you want to last, spending the extra money on premium polymeric sand is unquestionably worth it.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <strong>Mid-range brands (Sakrete, Quikrete)</strong> are widely available at big-box home improvement stores and offer solid performance at a moderate price point. They work well for standard residential projects and are a reasonable choice for budget-conscious homeowners who are willing to be diligent about proper installation technique. The polymer quality is generally good, though not as refined as the premium products.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <strong>Bargain and no-name brands</strong> should be approached with caution. Some substitute Portland cement for polymer binders, which creates a rigid, brittle joint that cracks under thermal expansion and is almost guaranteed to cause hazing. If the product is significantly cheaper than comparable bags from known brands, read the ingredient list carefully and look for independent reviews before purchasing.
                </p>

                <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Frequently Asked Questions
                </h2>

                <div class="space-y-6">
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much polymeric sand do I need?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Coverage depends on joint width, joint depth, and paver size. As a rough guide, a 50-pound bag of polymeric sand covers approximately <strong>30 to 60 square feet</strong> for standard pavers with 1/4-inch joints. Larger format pavers with wider joints require more material per square foot. For natural stone with irregular, wide joints, you may need twice as much. Always check the coverage chart on the specific product you are purchasing — they vary significantly between brands and formulations. Buy 10 to 15 percent more than your calculated need to account for waste and top-ups after compaction.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I apply polymeric sand to wet pavers?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Absolutely not. This is the single most common cause of installation failure. The pavers and joint cavities must be completely dry. Moisture activates the polymer prematurely, causing it to bind on the paver surface (creating haze) and preventing proper deep-joint bonding. If it rained recently, wait at least 24 to 48 hours of warm, dry weather before starting. In cool or humid conditions, wait even longer. When in doubt, tape a piece of plastic wrap over a joint and check after a few hours — if condensation appears under the plastic, the joints are still too wet.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I walk on polymeric sand before it dries?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">You can walk on the sand during the dry application phase (Steps 1 through 4) without any issues — you are walking on it as you sweep and compact. However, once you activate it with water in Step 5, you should keep all foot traffic off the surface for at least <strong>24 hours</strong>, and vehicle traffic off for at least <strong>72 hours</strong>. Walking on freshly activated joints disrupts the curing polymer and creates depressions and footprints that harden permanently.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does polymeric sand last?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">When properly installed under appropriate conditions, quality polymeric sand lasts between <strong>5 and 10 years</strong> before significant degradation occurs. In harsh climates with extreme freeze-thaw cycling, salt exposure, or heavy vehicle traffic, lifespan may be closer to 3 to 5 years. Factors that shorten lifespan include poor drainage that allows standing water on the paver surface, regular exposure to de-icing chemicals, and heavy power washing that gradually erodes the cured polymer. Regular light maintenance and avoiding unnecessary stress on the joints will maximize longevity.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I seal pavers before or after applying polymeric sand?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Apply polymeric sand first, allow it to fully cure for at least 30 days, and then apply paver sealer. Sealing before applying polymeric sand prevents the polymer from bonding to the paver edges within the joint, dramatically reducing hold strength. Sealing too soon after polymeric sand installation (before full cure) can trap moisture and uncured polymer beneath the sealer, causing whitening, cloudiness, and sealer failure. The 30-day wait between polymeric sand and sealer application is non-negotiable for best results.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use polymeric sand with natural stone pavers?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, but with extra caution. Natural stone (flagstone, bluestone, travertine, slate) is often more porous than manufactured concrete pavers, which means it absorbs polymeric sand particles and dissolved polymer more readily. This makes haze formation more likely and haze removal more difficult on natural stone. Use a premium brand specifically formulated for natural stone applications, and be extra thorough in your surface cleanup before water activation. Some very porous stones may benefit from a pre-seal with a penetrating stone sealer before polymeric sand application — check the sand manufacturer's recommendations for your specific stone type.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What if it rains right after I apply polymeric sand?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">If rain arrives before you have activated the sand with water, it will act as an uncontrolled activation — likely washing sand out of joints unevenly and causing haze as polymer-laden water flows across the paver surface. If rain arrives after controlled activation but within the first 24 hours of curing, the still-soft polymer can wash out of shallow joints. In either case, you may need to remove the compromised sand, let everything dry, and re-apply. This is why checking the weather forecast is Step Zero of any polymeric sand project. If sudden rain threatens, covering the surface with plastic sheeting can salvage the installation.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

